      "rev_n_4_1e3df1_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_1e3df1_1",
        "node_id": "n_4_1e3df1",
        "thought_text": "I apologize for the oversight. The error occurred because the indexing in the `superiors` list was incorrect. The correct indexing should be `superiors[i - 2]` instead of `superiors[i - 1]`. This mistake led to an IndexError when trying to access elements of the `superiors` list. Let's correct this and provide a brief explanation of the reasoning:\n\nThought-2 (corrected): The error was due to incorrect indexing in the `superiors` list. We need to use `superiors[i - 2]` to correctly map the officers to their superiors, as the list is 0-indexed but the officers are 1-indexed. This correction ensures that we're accessing the correct elements of the `superiors` list when building the tree structure.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2013315980307133110",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_4_1e3df1_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_afe4568269",
        "created_at": 1794528090.6016831
      },
      "rev_n_6_cfec8c_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_cfec8c_1",
        "node_id": "n_6_cfec8c",
        "thought_text": "The error in the previous implementation was due to an incorrect assumption about the order of command spreading. The DFS traversal doesn't guarantee the correct order of command spreading as described in the problem. Instead, we should use a level-order traversal (BFS) to correctly simulate the command spreading process. This will ensure that officers receive the command in the correct order, matching the example given in the problem statement.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2013315980307133110",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_6_cfec8c_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_b89fcf561e",
        "created_at": 1794528229.0256789
      },
      "rev_n_7_e5a334_0": {
